       "type": "example",
       "title": "Tutorial 01. short-answer questions: model answers",
       "bodyHtml": "<p><b>Q1. Define economics in your own words.</b></p>\n    \n    \n    \n\n    <p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><b>Q2. Explain the difference between needs and wants using an example.</b></p>\n    \n    \n    \n\n    <p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><b>Q3. Briefly explain the foundation concept of economics.</b></p>\n    \n    \n    \n\n    <p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><b>Q4. Explain opportunity cost using your own example.</b></p>\n    \n    \n    \n\n    <p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><b>Q5. List five statements under positive and normative economics.</b></p>",
-      "solutionHtml": "Economics is the social science that studies how people, firms and governments allocate their <b>limited (scarce) resources</b> among <b>competing alternative uses</b> in order to produce goods and services that satisfy <b>unlimited wants</b> and maximise welfare or gains. (Any answer capturing scarcity + choice + alternative uses earns full marks.)"
+      "solutionHtml": "<p><b>Q1:</b> Economics is the social science that studies how people, firms and governments allocate their <b>limited (scarce) resources</b> among <b>competing alternative uses</b> in order to produce goods and services that satisfy <b>unlimited wants</b> and maximise welfare or gains. (Any answer capturing scarcity + choice + alternative uses earns full marks.)</p>\n      <p><b>Q2:</b> A <b>need</b> is something required for basic survival (food, water, shelter); a <b>want</b> is a desire for a particular way of satisfying the need (pizza, bottled water, an apartment). Needs are limited; wants are unlimited — e.g. you need food but want pizza tonight and sushi tomorrow.</p>\n      <p><b>Q3:</b> The foundation concept is <b>scarcity</b>: resources are limited while wants are unlimited, which forces <b>choice</b>, and every choice has an <b>opportunity cost</b>. Scarcity → choice → opportunity cost is the chain on which the whole subject is built.</p>\n      <p><b>Q4 (example):</b> A student's evening could be spent working a part-time job earning Rs 1,500 or studying for the exam. Choosing to study costs the Rs 1,500 forgone — that forgone wage is the opportunity cost of the study time (the next-best alternative sacrificed).</p>\n      <p><b>Q5 (examples):</b> Positive: \"Sri Lanka's inflation was 6.8% last year\"; \"raising the sugar tax reduces consumption\"; \"unemployment is 5% of the labour force\". Normative: \"the government should control food prices\"; \"income inequality is too high\"; \"education should be free for all\". Positive = testable fact; normative = value judgement.</p>"
     },
     {
       "type": "quiz",
