+      "solutionHtml": "<p><b>Demand:</b> slope = ΔQ/ΔP = (0−16)/(10−2) = −2; using (10, 0): 0 = a − 20 → a = 20. &nbsp;<b>Q<sub>d</sub> = 20 − 2P</b></p>\n      <p><b>Supply:</b> slope = (10−(−6))/(10−2) = +2; using (10, 10): 10 = a + 20 → a = −10. &nbsp;<b>Q<sub>s</sub> = −10 + 2P</b></p>\n      <p><b>Equilibrium:</b> 20 − 2P = −10 + 2P → 30 = 4P → <b>P = 7.5</b>, <b>Q = 5</b>. (Matches the schedule: at P = 7.5 both demand and supply equal 5.) Graph: downward D through (2,16) and (10,0); upward S through (2,−6) and (10,10); they cross at (5, 7.5).</p>\n      <p><b>Exercise 2:</b> Qd = 3300 − 2P, Qs = 500 + 8P.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>3300 − 2P = 500 + 8P → 2800 = 10P → <b>P = 280</b></li>\n        <li><b>Q = 3300 − 2(280) = 2740</b> (check supply: 500 + 8(280) = 2740 ✓)</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><b>Tutorial Q2 (coffee supply):</b></p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Price of coffee increases → <b>movement up along</b> the supply curve (quantity supplied rises; no shift).</li>\n        <li>Subsidy to coffee farmers → costs fall → supply curve shifts <b>right</b>.</li>\n        <li>Fertiliser price increases → costs rise → supply shifts <b>left</b>.</li>\n        <li>New tax on producers → costs rise → supply shifts <b>left</b>.</li>\n        <li>Price of tea increases → farmers switch land to the more profitable tea → coffee supply shifts <b>left</b>.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p><b>Tutorial Q3 (rice market):</b> Qd = 350 − 10P, Qs = −40 + 5P.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>(i) 350 − 10P = −40 + 5P → 390 = 15P → <b>equilibrium price P = 26</b></li>\n        <li>(ii) <b>Q = 350 − 260 = 90</b> (check: −40 + 130 = 90 ✓)</li>\n        <li>(iii) Downward D from (0, 35) to (350, 0); upward S starting at P = 8; cross at (Q, P) = (90, 26) — the equilibrium chart above is drawn the same way.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p><b>Tutorial Q4 (market aggregation):</b> 80 consumers each Qd = 60 − 5P; 40 producers each Qs = −4 + 3P.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Market demand: Qd = 80 × (